# Struct Embedding in Go

Embedding is Go's mechanism for **composition** — including another type in a struct without an explicit field name. Methods from the embedded type are **promoted** to the outer type and can be called directly.

## Anonymous Fields (Embedding)

```go
type Animal struct { Name string }
func (a Animal) Breathe() string { return a.Name + " is breathing" }

type Dog struct {
    Animal        // embedding — no field name
    Breed string
}
func (d Dog) Bark() string { return d.Name + ": Woof!" }

dog := Dog{Animal: Animal{Name: "Rex"}, Breed: "Labrador"}
fmt.Println(dog.Breathe()) // promoted method
fmt.Println(dog.Name)      // promoted field
```

## Method Shadowing

If the outer type defines a method with the same name, it **shadows** the embedded method:

```go
type Cat struct { Animal; Indoor bool }

func (c Cat) Eat(food string) string {
    return c.Name + " delicately eats " + food
}

cat := Cat{Animal: Animal{Name: "Whiskers"}}
fmt.Println(cat.Eat("fish"))        // Cat's method
fmt.Println(cat.Animal.Eat("fish")) // Animal's method directly
```

## Embedding vs Inheritance

Embedding is **not inheritance**. Dog is not a subtype of Animal — it cannot be passed where an Animal is expected. Use interfaces for polymorphism:

```go
type Eater interface { Eat(string) string }
func feed(e Eater) { fmt.Println(e.Eat("kibble")) }
feed(dog) // OK — Dog has Eat from Animal
feed(cat) // OK — Cat has its own Eat
```

